# Deploy a workbook to Railway

Deploy a workbook to Railway.

app :railway__deploy_a_workbook_to_railway do
  title       "Deploy a workbook to Railway"
  description "Deploy a workbook to Railway"
end

### Ship a built workbook .html as a static site on the user's own Railway account, with the real railway CLI.

## When to use

The user wants their workbook live on the web hosted on *their own* Railway account (vs Cloudflare Pages, or our broker / Live URL). Railway is a good pick when they already use Railway for other services, or want the workbook to live alongside a backend they run there.

## Workflow

Use the real `railway` CLI directly (you, the agent, run it via bash). There is no `work forge web` command — that wrapper (and its `deploy-kit/web/railway.sh` recipe) was removed with the Rust `work` (2026-06-09). Railway routes to `$PORT`, so a static file needs a tiny server in front of it; the standard pattern is a small Caddy image.

```bash
## build the workbook if needed (emits dist/<slug>.html)
work build

## one-time per machine: browser OAuth into the USER'S Railway account
railway login                       # check with: railway whoami

## link a project (or `railway link` an existing one)
railway init --name <slug>

## stage the artifact + a Caddy image that binds Railway's $PORT
mkdir -p site && cp dist/<slug>.html site/index.html
cat > Caddyfile <<'EOF'
:{$PORT:80}
root * /site
file_server
EOF
cat > Dockerfile <<'EOF'
FROM caddy:2-alpine
COPY Caddyfile /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
COPY site /site
EOF

## build the Dockerfile + deploy; then mint a public URL if there's none yet
railway up
railway domain
```

## Auth — we set up THEIR CLI, we never hold the token

## Common pitfalls

- *$PORT*: Railway injects `$PORT` and routes to it. The Caddyfile above binds
`:{$PORT:80}`; a static server that hardcodes :80 fails Railway's health check.
- *Project linking*: `railway up` needs a linked project — =railway init --name
<slug>= when the cwd isn't linked yet, or `railway link` to target an existing project.
- *Build vs static*: deploying the Caddy Dockerfile (rather than leaning on
Railway's buildpack auto-detection) makes a bare HTML file deploy predictably.

## Verification checklist

- `railway whoami` shows the expected account.
- `railway status` shows the project + a successful deployment.
- The `railway domain` URL returns 200 and renders the workbook.

## See also

- `railway --help` and https://docs.railway.com/guides/cli — authoritative.
- https://docs.railway.com/guides/dockerfiles — Dockerfile deploys.